Cross-file const receivers do not get apply_receiver_const on the method return type, so downstream ConstViolation still misses cross-file


## Found by lane-enum while fixing B116, 2026-08-31. Explicitly out of B116's scope.

B116's same-file arm calls `apply_receiver_const` on the method return type.
The new cross-file arm deliberately does **not**.

Consequence: downstream `ConstViolation` diagnostics still miss the cross-file
case even though the const-receiver *call* check now works there. The two halves
of the same rule disagree about how far the const propagates.

## Why it was left out

The lane measured the call-check surface (827 unique sites, 807 excluded by
provenance, 17 genuine cross-file workspace classes, zero would-fire). It did
**not** measure the return-type propagation surface, and shipping an unmeasured
false-positive surface is the thing this batch keeps refusing to do.

So this is not a known-safe one-liner. **Measure first**, the same way B116 did:
instrument the widened propagation to keep the current answer while recording
every disagreement across the fleet, read the disagreements, then write the fix.

## What to watch for

The 807 engine/builtin receivers B116 excluded by provenance are the same
population here, and `lookup_method_return`'s "External types: no const-qualifier
metadata yet" comment applies to return types just as much as to receivers.
Whatever gate this uses must be structural — reading only the workspace half of
the merged view, as `workspace_own_method_is_const` does — rather than a filter
someone can forget to apply.

## Test axes

Pair every case same-file against cross-file and assert the two are EQUAL, which
is the shape B116 used and the reason its gaps are now visible rather than
silent. Cover a const method returning a handle, a value, a reference, and an
array; and both a const and a non-const overload of the same name, since the
overload set is where "any const overload makes the call legal" and "the return
type of the one that matched" can diverge.